Think of placing a drop of food coloring at one exact point in water.

Over time, the color spreads out.

Heat makes atomic movement easier, so higher temperatures generally accelerate diffusion.

The fab therefore wants enough heat to repair damage and activate dopants, but not so much heat for so long that the carefully engineered dopant profile blurs.

That tradeoff leads to one of today's most important concepts.

4. Thermal budget

Thermal budget means the total amount of heat exposure a wafer experiences across its manufacturing process.

It depends on both temperature and time.

A wafer held at a moderately high temperature for a long period can accumulate a large thermal budget.

A wafer heated to a very high temperature for only milliseconds can sometimes accomplish a desired reaction while limiting how far atoms have time to diffuse.

The exact physics is more complicated, but the investing intuition is simple:

As transistor dimensions shrink, fabs have less room for unwanted atomic movement.

That makes thermal control more valuable.

You should think of thermal budget as a scarce manufacturing allowance.

Every high-temperature step uses some of that allowance.

5. Traditional furnace processing

One way to heat wafers is in a furnace.

A batch furnace can process many wafers together at elevated temperature.

This has important economic advantages:

- many wafers can be processed at once;
- throughput per tool can be attractive;
- the equipment can support oxidation, diffusion, deposition and anneal applications.

Tokyo Electron's TELINDY PLUS platform, for example, is a 300mm batch furnace used for oxidation, anneal and LPCVD deposition.

ASM also sells vertical furnaces for thermal processes such as diffusion and oxidation.

Batch processing is useful, but it is not ideal for every thermal step.

If a process requires extremely short, tightly controlled heating, a single-wafer rapid thermal system can be better.

6. Rapid thermal processing

Rapid thermal processing, or RTP, heats one wafer very quickly and then cools it quickly.

Instead of slowly heating an entire batch inside a furnace, RTP can use powerful lamps or other heat sources to drive the wafer temperature upward at a much faster rate.

The goal is precise time-temperature control.

A typical advanced anneal may expose the wafer to high temperature for seconds or less rather than many minutes.

That lets the fab activate dopants while reducing the time available for diffusion.

Applied Materials is a major supplier of RTP equipment.

Its Vantage platform supports multiple anneal approaches, including lamp-based and laser-based processing.

The important point is not the brand name.

It is why customers pay for the equipment:

faster heating + tighter temperature control + faster cooling = more control over the transistor's final electrical properties.

7. Soak, spike and millisecond anneals

You will hear several anneal styles.

Soak anneal

The wafer reaches a target temperature and stays there for a meaningful period.

This gives reactions time to occur but uses more thermal budget.

Spike anneal

The wafer rapidly reaches a high temperature and is cooled almost immediately, creating a sharp temperature peak.

The idea is to get the activation benefit of high temperature while reducing diffusion time.

Millisecond anneal

The wafer surface is heated for only milliseconds.

Laser-based systems can deliver extremely intense, localized heating with extremely short exposure.

Applied Materials says its Vantage Astra laser anneal can heat the top surface layers above 1,000°C in less than a millisecond.

The exact recipe depends on the device and process step.

Do not memorize which anneal goes with which transistor yet.
